The C library's slabs come from shared chunks, not a mapping each.
frigg's slab_pool mapped every new slab on its own, 512 KiB to cut a
256 KiB slab out of with the alignment it needs, and a program that
does nothing still opens five (one for each size of object the
dynamic linker allocates): five mmaps, five regions in the kernel, and
five unmaps as it exits, in a program that is run and waited for every
time. Slabs are never unmapped, since the pool unmaps only the blocks
too large for a slab, so they need not be mappings of their own. The
pool now asks a policy that has map_slab() for each slab, with its
size and alignment, and mlibc's VirtualAllocator cuts them from
chunks of eight: one mmap per eight slabs, one region for them in the
kernel, and one unmap at exit. The address of the next slab and the
number the chunk has left live in one word, in the low bits that the
slabs' alignment leaves clear, taken with a compare-and-swap, so that
no lock is held that a fork's child could find held by a thread it
does not have; a thread that maps a chunk while another has put one in
gives back all but its first slab. The pool's other two ways to map a
slab stay for policies that do not have map_slab().

diff -ruN a/options/internal/generic/allocator.cpp b/options/internal/generic/allocator.cpp
--- a/options/internal/generic/allocator.cpp
+++ b/options/internal/generic/allocator.cpp
